  <p class="iop-essay-label">
    WEEK 06 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>
    3.141592654
  </h3>

  <blockquote>
    “Which is the better story?”
    <br>
    —Life of Pi
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Pass the Cool Whip—we’re having pie. Well, maybe not with my pie. My pie is anything but cool. Pieces of my pie are pretty bad, specifically Independence, Career and Finances, and Physical Health. The categories of Personal Growth, Spirituality, Creativity and Hobbies, Friends and Family, and Mental Health and Sobriety are doing very well.
  </p>

  <p>
    This pie is a SMART Recovery Lifestyle Balance Pie. It’s an exercise designed to evaluate satisfaction across different areas of life. It’s about how fulfilling these categories are. It’s not expected that all of the categories will score perfect tens or even score high numbers. Life balance isn’t about having eight equally perfect pie slices. It’s about understanding how the slices influence one another, protecting what’s working, and making improvements where they’re needed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let’s dig into my results.
  </p>

  <!-- LIFESTYLE BALANCE PIE IMAGE -->
  <figure class="iop-essay-image">
    <%= image_tag "pie.jpg",
        alt: "SMART Recovery Lifestyle Balance Pie",
        loading: "lazy" %>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Starting with Career and Finances, this scored very low. I am currently frustrated that I cannot find a job no matter how hard I try. I have revised my resume countless times, tried a lot of different things and ways to find and obtain a job—yet nothing works. I have also tried to build a network, only to hear crickets and see tumbleweed rolling by. It seems as if all the hours spent on this are wasted, and I will never find employment. And finances—what finances? I have no independence.
  </p>

  <p>
    Physical health for me is a joke. Severe spinal stenosis and degenerative disc disease are my official back diagnoses, as well as arthritis and bone spurs on my spine. I have to have spine surgery at age 35, and I am told frequently that I am “too young for this,” as if I don’t already know this. I have very limited mobility, I am currently mostly housebound, I walk with a cane, and I consider going grocery shopping for 10–15 minutes exhilarating. I cannot run, cycle, skateboard, or do any of the hobbies I enjoy. It takes me 20 minutes to clean my bathroom. I am sick of being a prisoner to my limitations. Overall, I am tired. My foot is numb, my back aches from just existing, and I am over it. I have no independence.
  </p>

  <p>
    Lastly, independence. Which I have none of. I have no car because I was irresponsible and did not manage mania properly, which led to getting a DUI. I totaled my car and lost my license. I have to get rides on someone else’s schedule. As stated above, I am confined to my apartment 95% of the time. I am an inmate in my own body. The only true freedom I have is how I spend my time, whether I’m sober, and what I eat. Otherwise, I have zero control over anything. I am having back surgery, and I couldn’t even pick my date—I had to pick my date based on someone else’s schedule. My health was set up on someone else’s schedule. My mental illness has also stolen my independence—I am completely dependent on my medications, and I have limitations because of bipolar disorder, ADHD, other issues, and PTSD. It’s obnoxious, tiring, annoying, and I’m over it.
  </p>

  <p>
    There’s a trend here: loss of independence. There are a couple of fixes that will happen eventually, but living with this situation is very difficult. If I had a job, I would have income, which would give me independence, but I need a car to get a job. If I had a functioning back, I could go out and do physical activities like ride my bike, but I need a car to drive to places to ride my bike. If I were physically healthy, I could go to work and not worry about back issues, but I am not physically healthy. And I still need a car to get to work. Thus I have no independence… and ad nauseam. All of these affect each other. The loss of independence due to a couple of things throws a wrench into the entire concept of independence.
  </p>

  <p>
    So, what’s going right? Family, Mental Health and Sobriety, Creativity and Hobbies, Spirituality, and Personal Growth. These are also connected to each other, just as the others are. They intertwine to create positive connections.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, Creativity and Hobbies and Spirituality are connected to Mental Health and Sobriety and Personal Growth. The former offer ways to relieve stress, express emotions, find fulfillment, and develop new skills; spirituality provides a sense of purpose, mindfulness, and acceptance during difficult times. Both contribute to emotional well-being and personal growth by providing healthy outlets and opportunities for self-reflection. Friends and Family are linked to Mental Health and Sobriety as well. They offer emotional support, encouragement, companionship, and a sense of belonging. These relationships are particularly important when dealing with physical limitations, unemployment, and a lack of independence. The stronger areas of my life don’t eliminate the problems in the weaker ones, but they help me manage those difficulties without becoming completely overwhelmed by them. Rather than functioning as isolated slices, these categories support and strengthen one another, demonstrating that life balance is about more than simply achieving equal satisfaction in every area.
  </p>

  <p>
    The highs and the lows are there—so now what do I do? The weak areas are identified, so it’s time to come up with a plan. Recognizing an imbalance is useless without coming up with a plan for it. Setting realistic, achievable goals is the next step for each area with a low score:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Career: Review my resume, continue reaching out and applying for jobs while accepting that the hiring process is out of my control.</li>
    <li>Physical health: Follow my doctor’s orders until my surgery.</li>
    <li>Independence: Find practical ways to become less reliant on others, or do things to exercise independence in my own ways and get that feeling.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Progress on these goals is also going to be gradual—it’s not going to happen overnight. It will take work every day to make these things happen. Persistence is key here. Improvement doesn’t always happen on the desired timeline, and slow progress isn’t necessarily failure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another useful tool from SMART Recovery is the Hierarchy of Values, which encourages identifying the five most important things in life and examining whether everyday decisions reflect those priorities. There can be a significant difference between what is considered important and what is actually prioritized through actions. For example, sobriety, friendships, creativity, and spirituality are all areas of my life that contribute to my mental health, personal growth, and overall satisfaction. However, during active addiction, drinking sometimes took precedence over the very things I valued, even when it meant jeopardizing my relationships, health, freedom, and independence. Now, my priorities are better reflected in my everyday decisions. Maintaining sobriety, nurturing relationships, engaging in creative activities, and practicing spirituality contribute to a more fulfilling life, even while my career, physical health, and independence remain unsatisfactory. The Hierarchy of Values demonstrates that personal fulfillment isn't necessarily measured by financial success, professional achievements, or complete independence, but by how closely actions align with what matters most. A balanced life isn't about having everything figured out; it's about making choices that support personal values rather than undermine them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Friends and Family got a perfect score. I have an amazing support system, and a lot of people help me. Family and neighbors give me rides. Friends text and call. Group therapy peers care and check in. They are all concerned about my spinal issues and care about my sobriety, celebrating milestones with me. I am very dependent on them now. This won’t change when I gain my independence back, but I will need to remember that being independent doesn’t necessarily mean doing everything alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another thought is whether balance should be defined by equal satisfaction across every category. I would say no, because some areas of life naturally require more attention at different times. Mental Health and Sobriety should always be a high priority—for me, they are Priority 1. However, Friends and Family might become Priority 1 if someone gets sick. Currently, Physical Health takes priority over my career. The takeaway here is that balance isn’t a fixed state: it changes according to circumstances, needs, and priorities.
  </p>

  <p>
    To address these issues, using SMART goals is the way to go. SMART stands for:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Specific: Clearly define what you want to accomplish.</li>
    <li>Measurable: Establish how you'll track progress.</li>
    <li>Achievable: Choose something realistic.</li>
    <li>Relevant: Make sure it aligns with your values and priorities.</li>
    <li>Time-bound: Establish a timeframe.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These bullet points are a reliable way to come up with a goal that is realistic and attainable. For the different low-scoring pie pieces, here are the goals:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Career and Finances: Establish a manageable job-search routine and track applications, follow-ups, and opportunities.</li>
    <li>Physical Health: Focus on treatment, recovery, and achievable improvements in mobility.</li>
    <li>Independence: Do things that increase my sense of independence and work toward regaining greater autonomy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The goals should acknowledge what’s in my control and what isn’t.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pie isn’t about how to measure up life to a perfect 10. I’m not looking for the judges at the gymnastics event to all hold up ‘10’ signs for me. My Career and Finances, Physical Health, and Independence may be struggling now, but that can change. Life isn’t fixed—it’s fluid. And just because those scores were low on the pie, it doesn’t mean I don’t find satisfaction in my relationships, sobriety, creativity, spirituality, and personal growth. There are circumstances beyond my control that I practice radical acceptance about, and other situations take time, effort, and persistence to improve. My goal isn’t to turn every category into a perfect ten but to recognize what matters, what needs improvement, prioritize accordingly, and make appropriate decisions that reflect my values. Balance isn’t a permanent destination where everything magically falls into place: it’s an ongoing process of adapting to changing circumstances, maintaining what’s working, and improving on what isn’t. I may not have all pretty pie pieces now, but pass that Cool Whip anyway—we can just cover the less pretty pieces and enjoy the pie regardless.
  </p>

</article>
